{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfyjuAWfzw2jtbjRxvEui","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A **mutex** gives one thread exclusive access to something. A **semaphore** is a counter that limits how many threads can do something at once, and it's also used for signaling between threads.\n\n## Mutex (mutual exclusion lock)\n- **Two states:** locked or unlocked.\n- **It has an owner.** Only the thread that locked it can unlock it. That ownership lets some implementations offer extra features, such as priority inheritance (which avoids priority inversion), recursive locking and error checking.\n- **Use it to protect a critical section,** for example shared data that only one thread may change at a time.\n\n```c\npthread_mutex_lock(&m);\nbalance += amount;   // only one thread here at a time\npthread_mutex_unlock(&m);\n```\n\n## Semaphore\n- **It holds an integer count.** `wait` (also called P or down) decrements it and blocks if the count is 0. `signal` (also called V or up) increments it and wakes a waiting thread.\n- **It has no owner.** Any thread can signal it, even one that never waited on it.\n- **Two kinds:**\n  - **Counting semaphore:** allows up to N threads at once, for example a pool of 5 database connections.\n  - **Binary semaphore:** the count is only 0 or 1. It looks like a mutex but has no ownership.\n- **Use it to limit access to a pool of resources, or for signaling,** for example when a producer tells a consumer \"an item is ready.\"\n\n```c\nsem_wait(&slots);    // block if all N slots are in use\nuse_connection();\nsem_post(&slots);\n```\n\n## Key differences\n\n| | Mutex | Semaphore |\n|---|---|---|\n| Value | Locked / unlocked | Integer count (0..N) |\n| Ownership | Yes: the thread that locks it must unlock it | No: any thread can signal |\n| Concurrent holders | 1 | Up to N |\n| Main purpose | Mutual exclusion | Limiting access to a resource pool, or signaling |\n| Priority inheritance | Often supported | Generally not |\n\n## Rule of thumb\n- To protect shared data, use a **mutex**.\n- To allow N threads at once, or for one thread to notify another, use a **semaphore**.\n\nA common mistake is to use a binary semaphore as a lock. It works, but you lose ownership checks and priority inheritance. Nothing stops another thread from releasing your \"lock\" by accident."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12080,"cache_read_input_tokens":25083,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12080},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff01f16c-7b3f-5ea5-8051-7a95ac7cd1d1","uuid":"1557228a-0c91-4d90-9931-bfa79cfeb33f","timestamp":"2026-10-13T06:11:24.083Z","request_id":"req_011CfyjuAJXEJaaVzh4S5WBu"}
